Whitehaven on the coast of West Cumbria  the location of a bitter and  increasingly political row over the UK's  first deep Coal Mine in three decades  the proposed Woodhouse colliery would  sit here next to the town on an old  industrial site galey Stevens is just  one of the many campaigners opposing it  it's wrong  the people of Cumbria the people of  Whitehaven deserve better than to be  taken back into the past Whitehaven  should be making  solar panels and new ways of doing  things but instead they're trying to  sell the local people on the idea that  there's money and there are jobs around  coal  this is a rendering of what's planned  it's taken years of wrangling but now  the government has just a few weeks to  make a final decision on whether or not  to go ahead  local Mayor Mike Starkey can see only  Economic Opportunity the coal would be  for steel making he says a booming  industry I would welcome any Renewables  coming into this border whether you want  a nuclear reactor a solar panel a wind  turbine they all need substantial  amounts of Steel and the only way of  manufacturing that steel is you need  gold King Cole  mining has been in my blood since 1964.  Dave Craddock comes from a proud family  of minors for him digging again is about  preserving a precious cultural identity  and its Common Sense our own source of  energy is beneath our feet you produce  power from what you've got not from  somebody else transporting it here  it's not clear how much of the coal  would be for domestic use or export  either way the decision is looming  this mine represents a really thorny set  of problems facing government ministers  on the one hand they've got the leveling  up agenda which means small investment  in the north of the country and they  want to increase our energy Independence  but on the other the UK is supposed to  be a leader on climate change and it's  been pushing other countries to stop  using coal which is the dirtiest of all  fossil fuels  for this reason the government's own  climate change advisors have said that a  decision to open a new coal mine would  be unacceptable  but a looming recession amidst a global  energy crisis might change the  calculation in the uncertainty of this  political moment Anything is Possible  Hannah Thomas Peter Sky News in West  Cumbria
 

